 School Management System – Microservices

This project converts the original monolithic
[School Management System](https://github.com/rsroshini01-art/school-management-system)
(Java, Spring Boot, MySQL) into a **Spring Boot microservices architecture**.
Every module of the original project now runs as its own service with its own database.
The services are registered in **Eureka**, read their settings from a **Config Server**
and are reached through one **API Gateway** that checks the login token (JWT).

Architecture

                         Postman / Client
                                |
                                v
                  +-----------------------------+
                  |  API Gateway  (port 8080)   |  <- checks the JWT token
                  +-----------------------------+
                                |  finds services by name
                                v
                  +-----------------------------+
                  |  Eureka Server (port 8761)  |  <- service registry
                  +-----------------------------+
                                ^
     all services register here |      all services read settings from
                                |
                  +-----------------------------+
                  | Config Server (port 8888)   |
                  +-----------------------------+

  academic-service   auth-service     student-service   teacher-service
  attendance-service exam-service     fee-service       communication-service
     (each one has its own MySQL database)

 Services

| Service | Port | Database | What it does |

| eureka-server | 8761 | – | Service registry (every service registers here) |
| config-server | 8888 | – | Central configuration (database settings, JWT settings) |
| api-gateway | 8080 | – | Single entry point, routes requests, validates the JWT token |
| academic-service | 8081 | academic_db | Schools, academic years, terms, classes, sections, subjects |
| auth-service | 8082 | auth_db | Register, login, refresh token, logout, users and roles |
| student-service | 8083 | student_db | Students, parents, enrollments, CSV import, student report |
| teacher-service | 8084 | teacher_db | Teachers, teacher-class assignment, timetable |
| attendance-service | 8085 | attendance_db | Attendance marking, attendance report |
| exam-service | 8086 | exam_db | Exams, exam subjects, marks and grades, grade rules, results, report card PDF, homework |
| fee-service | 8087 | fee_db | Fee structures, fee payments, PDF receipt, fee report |
| communication-service | 8088 | communication_db | Announcements, notifications, document upload and download |

 Technologies

- Java, Spring Boot 4.1
- Spring Cloud: Eureka (service discovery), Config Server, Gateway, OpenFeign
- Spring Data JPA, MySQL
- JWT (jjwt) for login tokens, validated at the Gateway
- iText for PDF generation (report card and receipt)
- Lombok, Maven

How the services talk to each other

Each service owns its own database. A service never reads another service's tables.
Instead, other modules are referenced by **id**, and a service asks the owner service
through **OpenFeign** (found by name in Eureka) when it needs more:

| Caller | Asks | Why |

| attendance-service | student-service | Check that the student exists and show the student name |
| student-service | academic-service | Check that the class exists and show the class name |
| fee-service | student-service | Check the student for a payment, show the name on the receipt |
| exam-service | student-service | Check the student for marks, results and report card |
| exam-service | academic-service | Show subject names in results and report card |

How to run

Run the applications in this order and wait for "Started ..." each time:

1. MySQL (create the 8 databases first, see below)
2. `eureka-server` (8761)
3. `config-server` (8888)
4. `auth-service`, `academic-service`, `student-service`, `teacher-service`,
   `attendance-service`, `exam-service`, `fee-service`, `communication-service`
5. `api-gateway` (8080) last

Open `http://localhost:8761` to check that all applications are registered.

**Databases to create in MySQL:**

 sql
CREATE DATABASE academic_db;
CREATE DATABASE auth_db;
CREATE DATABASE student_db;
CREATE DATABASE teacher_db;
CREATE DATABASE attendance_db;
CREATE DATABASE exam_db;
CREATE DATABASE fee_db;
CREATE DATABASE communication_db;
```

Tables are created automatically when each service starts.
The database username and password for each service are in
`config-server/.../src/main/resources/config/<service-name>.properties`.
Change them to match your own MySQL login.

Using the API through the Gateway

All calls go to the Gateway on port 8080, with the service name in front of the path:


http://localhost:8080/<service-name>/<path>

1. Register: `POST /auth-service/api/auth/register` (public)
2. Login: `POST /auth-service/api/auth/login` (public) – returns a token
3. Every other call needs the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`

Examples:

GET  http://localhost:8080/student-service/api/students
GET  http://localhost:8080/academic-service/api/classes?academicYearId=1
POST http://localhost:8080/attendance-service/api/attendance
GET  http://localhost:8080/exam-service/api/results/student/1/exam/1
GET  http://localhost:8080/exam-service/api/report-cards/student/1/exam/1   (PDF)
GET  http://localhost:8080/fee-service/api/fee-payments/1/receipt           (PDF)
```

A request without a token, or with a wrong or expired token, is rejected with **401**.

Reports

The reports of the old project are now spread over the services that own the data:

| Report | URL |

| Students | `/student-service/api/reports/students` |
| Attendance | `/attendance-service/api/reports/attendance` |
| Exams | `/exam-service/api/reports/exams?examId=1` |
| Fees | `/fee-service/api/reports/fees` |


Notes and limitations

- The role rules of the old project (`@PreAuthorize`) are not enforced yet. The Gateway
  checks that the token is valid, but does not check the user's role.
- A logged-out token is still accepted by the Gateway until it expires, because the
  token blacklist belongs to auth-service.
- The services can also be called directly on their own ports. In a real deployment only
  the Gateway would be exposed.
- Existence checks across services are done for the main links (student, class, subject
  names). Some other ids (for example section id, teacher id, academic year id in an exam)
  are stored as ids without being checked yet.
- The database passwords and JWT secrets in the config files are for local learning only.
